        {
          "dimension": "plan_feasibility_and_contract_objective",
          "fact": "The replacement abandons a proven nine-trick club route and spends the entries needed to use it.",
          "explanation": "Both defenders followed to CA and CK, leaving only CJ outstanding. A club to North's CQ necessarily removes CJ; North can return a heart to HA, allowing South to cash the two remaining clubs. Together with DK, HA, HK and SA, this supplies nine total tricks. Cashing those side winners first removes South's entries after CQ, while the stated major-suit development supplies no concrete replacement route.",
          "quotes": [
            "cash the remaining DK and the heart and spade aces/honors that are sure winners.",
            "lead toward CQ only if later play makes it safe.",
            "Seek the remaining tricks by developing the major suits while guarding against conceding the lead prematurely."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "decision-0008",
      "status": "fail",
      "findings": [
        {
          "dimension": "retained_plan_feasibility",
          "fact": "Keeping the replacement retains its defective ordering and unsupported avoidance of clubs.",
          "explanation": "D5 is a legal follow under DK, but the active advice still cashes the heart and spade winners before using CQ. Authorized club history leaves only CJ outstanding, so a club to CQ followed by a heart to HA provides access to South's established clubs and nine total tricks. Spending those entries first defeats that route, and the proposed major-suit development supplies no concrete alternative.",
